{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Latent robustness gap found by the LLM code review of Gerrit 1905 (C4).\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`scripts/gen_config_reference.py::_lifecycle_index()` uses `dict.setdefault` when mapping a\ncanonical schema key to its lifecycle note, so if one canonical replacement key is ever the\ntarget of MORE THAN ONE cfg deprecation alias and/or tombstone, only the first-encountered\nnote survives and the rest are silently dropped from the generated `docs/config-reference.md`.\nConfirmed in code: both loops in `_lifecycle_index` call `index.setdefault(replacement, ...)`.\n\nNo collision exists today (each cfg replacement key is targeted exactly once across\n`rebar._deprecations.REGISTRY` + `tombstones()`), so current generated output is correct.\nThis is a defensive fix that stops a future deprecation from silently deleting doc content.\n\n## Approach\n\nAccumulate the notes per canonical key instead of `setdefault`: collect them into a list keyed\nby replacement, then join them into the single `Lifecycle` table cell. The single-note case\nmust render byte-identically to today so `docs/config-reference.md` needs no regeneration and\nthe committed-tree drift oracle stays green.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` — rewrite `_lifecycle_index` to accumulate.\n- `tests/unit/test_gen_config_reference.py` — add the held-out collision guards.\n\nNo other module reads `_lifecycle_index`; `docs/env-vars.md` is out of scope because its\ngenerator only scans `src/rebar`.\n\n## Testing\n\nHeld-out guards seed collisions by monkeypatching the generator's `_cfg_deprecations` /\n`_cfg_tombstones` accessors onto a real schema key, then assert both notes survive into that\nkey's rendered table row:\n\n- Two deprecated aliases sharing one replacement — both alias names appear in the row.\n- One deprecated alias plus one tombstone sharing one replacement — both notes appear.\n- Each guard is confirmed RED against unmodified `main` (the second note is absent) before\n  the fix lands.\n- Regression: the whole existing oracle, including the read-only committed-tree `--check`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `_lifecycle_index` accumulates every lifecycle note for a canonical key with no silent\n  drop; checked by `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_gen_config_reference.py -q` passing the\n  new collision guards.\n- [ ] A test seeds a 2-alias collision and asserts both notes appear in that key's\n  `config-reference.md` row; checked by the same pytest run, and by observing it FAIL on\n  unmodified `main` first.\n- [ ] `python scripts/gen_config_reference.py --check` exits 0 against the committed tree and\n  the pre-existing oracle stays green; checked by that command plus the full\n  `tests/unit/test_gen_config_reference.py` run.\n- [ ] Gates stay green; checked by `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and\n  `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` reporting new=0 increased=0 stale=0."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787193636523455001,"uuid":"2ac326fc-a4ad-4628-a3a8-8ac03b0ba6e5"}